30 minute workouts 20 minute recipes 10  minute mindfulness ready to become get  fear on the App Store or Google Play  weight training is extremely essential  to your overall workout program when  done correctly weight training can help  you lose fat increase your metabolism to  burn more calories increase your  strength and muscle tone increase bone  density and improve your overall  appearance ladies if you want to achieve  the toned and strong look you got to  ditch at the cardio machine and start  lifting weights I do understand that it  can get very confusing and overwhelming  to some so I'm gonna share with you the  six principles to increase muscle to  lean up and lose weight first technique  I've always emphasized on proper form  and technique  having good technique when exercising is  extremely important because only then  will you be able to achieve better  results target the right muscles and  you're less likely to hurt yourself so  unless you suffer from a previous injury  you will always want to execute the full  range of motion in an exercise to  recruit the entire targeted muscle for  instance if you were to perform a half  squat instead of a full squat you will  not fully recruit your quads glutes and  hamstrings hence you're not training at  your optimum let's look at the mind-body  connection it is important to focus your  mind on the targeted muscle when  performing an exercise if you're a  beginner you may struggle with this  initially for instance when performing a  chest press instead of filling the chest  muscles you will feel more on your  shoulders this will take time but you  got to keep telling your mind to focus  on that particular chest muscle and  eventually you will start to create the  mind-body connection  second mechanical tension mechanical  tension is the primary stimulus for  muscle growth during strength training  muscles experience stretching forces  when they try to shorten but are  resisted when they do so they also  experience stretching forces when they  lengthen  while we are holding a load but these  forces are comparatively  if your focus is to increase strength  then the weights must be heavy enough  for you to only perform between four to  six repetitions max and keep between two  to five minutes rest interval however if  your focus is muscle hypertrophy or  muscle growth then the weights must be  heavy enough for you to perform between  6 to 12 rep max per set and take between  60 to 90 seconds rest interval if you  can lift more than your aunt lifting  heavy enough you've got to add on more  weights yes ladies you've got to lift  heavy to increase strength and lean up  and no it will not make you bulky but  muscle fatigue you got to feel like if  push yourself hard enough and in fact  you should train to failure what does  that mean muscle failure during strength  training is the point at which fatigue  is high enough to prevent a muscle from  exerting at the amount of force needed  to complete the current repetition with  the given load if doing another  repetition is possible you haven't  reached muscle failure the objective is  to induce the most possible muscle  growth by pumping maximum blood to the  area another way to target muscle  fatigue is to increase the volume per  muscle group this is basically  calculated based on the number of  exercises for one muscle group  multiplied by the number of sets for  each exercise for instance if we were to  work on our glutes and perform three  exercises hip thrusts Romanian deadlift  and kickbacks and we were to do three  sets for each workout the volume for the  workout will be three exercises  multiplied by three sets which means  nine a good volume to increase muscle  growth is between six to ten the volume  of exercise per muscle group is to be  gradually progressed according to your  training capability fourth principle  eccentric training an eccentric movement  is the lowering part of a move it's when  your muscle works as its lengthened for  example when you're lowering into a  squat or as you're lowering your arms in  a bicep curl the  or pushing of the weight would be  concentric eccentric training is much  more demanding on the muscles and  therefore if it takes them far more than  you could concentric Li this promotes  greater muscle damage greatest  stimulation and muscle growth as a  general rule of thumb for eccentric  training take 4 to 5 seconds to lower  the weight for instance take 4 seconds  to lower into a squat to lower the bar  to your chest or to lower the dumbbells  during a shoulder press  this then leads on to my next point  repetition tempo I'm sure you must have  come across some workout programs with  four digits written on them some of you  might be wondering what does each number  represent the first number represents  eccentric face followed by transition  concentric face and followed by  isometric why is this important  let's first understand time under  tension or more commonly referred to as  tu tee time under tension is the time  your muscle spends under load during a  set this includes the time spent in the  concentric isometric and eccentric face  for example if you perform at a mihrab  set and each rep only takes 3 seconds to  complete your muscle experiences 30  seconds of time under tension now if you  were to perform the same set but spend  two seconds lifting the weight one  second isometric and three seconds  lowering the weight then those same 10  reps would give you approximately 60  seconds of time under tension even  though the rep count stayed the same the  muscle spent significantly more time  under tension and that extra time  actually translates to a lot more work  remember  your muscle can't count they simply feel  the load created by the weight and the  mechanical pension that comes from  contracting the muscle under the load  therefore to increase the work done by  your muscles you can either increase the  load or increase at the time the muscle  is placed under this load here are some  of the common tempos used in a workout  program  and the final principle is recovery I'm  sure you might have come across some  people who would spend many hours in the  gym every single day  however spending more hours in the gym  does not equal to faster results because  your muscles need to recover in order to  grow and for you to trim at your optimum  level recovery can include stretching  right after a strength training session  during a lifting session the muscles you  use contract making them tighter not  stretching your muscles may lead to  decrease in range of motion and  flexibility which may also lead to  long-term injuries regular stretching  helps to increase circulation and  maintain flexibility so that you can  continue to train at your best recovery  also includes filling your body with  enough high-quality food no matter how  hard you train or how heavy you lift if  your diet is crap you're pretty much  wasting your efforts in the gym so make  sure you feed your muscles with enough  lean proteins cups vegetables and  healthy fats you will also need to  schedule in your rest days to avoid  overtraining so check in with yourself  consistently and ask yourself how's your  energy level how's it your stress level  and how's the sleep quality all these  would have a direct effect to your  recovery another important point is to  avoid under eating or consuming too  little carbs yes you may want to reduce  on your cups but your bodies still need  cups for energy if you're on an  extremely low carb diet that's when your  energy level dips your stress level  increases and this will also affect your  sleep and training if you have been  doing regular strength training and you  would like to improve or if you have hit  a plateau and struggled to progress then  incorporate these tips if you find that  you are learning from my videos and then  be sure to hit the subscribe button ok  share this video on give me a 